<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kinda</h1><p class="st-meta">425 CE – 540 CE (approx.) · capital Qaryat al-Faw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A central Arabian tribal kingdom that briefly united the Bedouin of Najd under Himyarite patronage and produced the poet Imru al-Qays.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kinda was a tribe from the borders of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a> that moved into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. In the 5th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom</a> installed its chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hujr-akil-al-murar">Hujr Akil al-Murar</a> as king over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maadd">Ma'add</a> tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, giving Himyar indirect control of the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaryat-al-faw">Qaryat al-Faw</a>, a caravan town on the edge of the Empty Quarter, is often linked with the kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kindite kings ruled as overlords of shifting tribal alliances rather than through fixed institutions, collecting tribute and leading raids. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-harith-ibn-amr">al-Harith ibn Amr</a> in the early 6th century, Kinda reached its peak and even held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">al-Hira</a> for a time at the expense of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a>, reportedly installed there by the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-harith-ibn-amr">al-Harith</a> was killed around 528, his sons divided the tribes and were defeated by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmids</a> and rival tribes. The kingdom had collapsed by about 540. Its memory survives through the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imru-al-qais">Imru al-Qays</a>, son of al-Harith's son Hujr, whose ode is one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muallaqat">Mu'allaqat</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kinda.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kinda" width="320" height="258"><figcaption>Image: Al Ameer son · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kindite_Kingdom,_ca._500.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>425 CE – 540 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qaryat al-Faw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hujr Akil al-Murar, al-Harith ibn Amr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kindah, Kindites, Kingdom of Kindah</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=482&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kinda" aria-label="Kingdom of Kinda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5899 1445 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kinda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6219 1701L6215 1905M6219 1701L6145 1273"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6215 1905h0M6145 1273h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6219 1701h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6219 1701h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Qaryat al-Faw. <a href="/atlas?year=482&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kinda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

